{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>The Me'il.</b> Rashi wrote (Rashi on Exodus 28:4) that it is like a kind of tunic, and so is the kuttonet, except that the kuttonet is worn next to the skin while the me'il he calls the upper tunic. But this is not correct, for the me'il is a garment one wraps oneself in, as it says (1 Samuel 28:14), \"and he is wrapped (עוֹטֶה) in a me'il,\" and it is written (Isaiah 61:10), \"He has wrapped me (יְעָטָנִי) with a me'il of righteousness,\" and the term wrapping (עֲטִיָּה) does not apply to the kuttonet but only to the salmah with which one covers oneself, as it is written (Psalms 104:2), \"Who wraps Himself (עוֹטֶה) in light like a salmah,\" for that is wrapping, and likewise \"he shall wrap himself (יַעְטֶה) above his lip\" (Leviticus 13:45), meaning he shall wrap himself. This is the translation of Jonathan ben Uzziel, which the Rav mentioned (Rashi above on verse 4): kardotin, for it is a wrapping close to the shape of the ephod, in which one wraps the lower half of the body toward the legs; for if it were a tunic they would not resemble each other at all. Further proof is what it says (1 Samuel 15:27), \"and he took hold of the wing (כְּנַף) of his me'il and tore it\" — now it has wings, so it is not like a tunic. Rather, the me'il is a garment that encircles the entire body from the neck downward to a person's feet, and it has no sleeves at all. And in other me'ilot it has a neck-opening opposite the entire neck sewn into it, and that neck-opening is called the opening of the me'il (פִּי מְעִיל), and Scripture commanded that it be woven together with it. The me'il is split entirely in the front from top to bottom; one inserts one's head through the hole of its opening, and the neck ends up encircled by the opening of the me'il, and in front are two wings with which he covers and uncovers at will — like a kind of cloak that has no neck-hole, where the stitching in the front is divided from the top of the collar downward; therefore the term wrapping (עֲטִיָּה) always applies to it.\n\nI also do not know why the Rav made the bells independent — a bell between two pomegranates — for if so, the pomegranates served no function at all, and if for ornament, why were they made as hollow pomegranates? He should have made them like golden apples. Furthermore, Scripture would have needed to explain on what the bells are hung, if he were to make rings on them from which to hang them. Rather, they are literally inside them: the pomegranates are hollow and made like small pomegranates whose mouths have not opened, and the bells are hidden inside them and visible through them. Scripture did not specify their number, but our Sages said that there were seventy-two pomegranates (זַגִּין), and in them seventy-two clappers (עִנְבָּלִין), and he hangs thirty-six on one side and thirty-six on the other, as is in Zevahim in the chapter The Altar (88a). From this you may learn that it is not like a tunic or a kuttonet, but rather it has wings.\n\nRashi also wrote (Rashi on Exodus 28:4) that the mitsnefet is like a hat (כּוֹבַע), since elsewhere (Exodus 28:40) it calls them migba'ot, and they translate as \"hats\" (קוֹבְעִין). This too is not so, for they said that the length of the mitsnefet is sixteen cubits, and it is like a kind of turban (צָנִיף) with which one wraps one's head, rolling and turning, rolling and turning around one's head, fold upon fold. The mitsnefet of the High Priest is never called a migba'at anywhere; but concerning an ordinary priest Scripture says migba'ot (below 28:40; 29:9, etc.), and that too is a mitsnefet, except that he ties it over his entire head and raises the folds on it like a kind of hat that one fits, according to the words of Onkelos — for migba'at is like miqba'at (a fitting-cap), as I said in the portion of Vayehi Miqqetz (Genesis 41:47), with the interchange of the letters gimel and qof. But the migba'at is like the mitsnefet, and therefore the Sages regularly mentioned, for both the High Priest and the ordinary priest, mitsnefet, in Torat Kohanim (Tzav 2:1). And in tractate Yoma (71b) we learned: the High Priest serves with eight garments and the ordinary priest with four — kuttonet, michnasayim, mitsnefet, and avnet; the High Priest has added to them the choshen, the ephod, the me'il, and the tzitz."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes Rashi's citation of Targum Jonathan's term kardotin from Rashi on Exodus 28:4; variant spellings in manuscripts (כדרוניא / כרדונין) are noted parenthetically in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:17:41.841300591Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}